--
C++23 std::expected<int, int> basic usage (construction, has_value,
operator*), real libstdc++ <expected>.

Was KNOWNBUG ("conversion from 'void' to 'signed int'" on *e): the VOID
partial specialization `expected<_Tp, _Er> requires is_void_v<_Tp>` was
selected for _Tp = int.  Its requires-clause substituted and simplified
to a NUMERIC zero constant (c-bool 0) rather than a Boolean `false`, and
the satisfaction check only tested is_false() -- so the unsatisfied
clause counted as satisfied (N5008 [temp.constr.atomic]/3) and the void
specialization (whose operator* returns void) shadowed the primary.
Fixed by also treating a zero constant as unsatisfied.  A header-free
mimic (integral_constant/false_type/is_void_v chain) does NOT reproduce
the numeric-zero shape; only the full libstdc++ chain does, so this
with-headers test is the regression test for the fix.
